  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nCurrent WV satellite imagery along with RAOB data show the \nWesterlies in a mostly zonal flow regime across southern Canada.  A \nsemi-permanent anticyclone continues to remain in place over the \nwestern CONUS into the Desert Southwest. Some flattening on the \nnorthern flank of this dome of high pressure has allowed for a weak \nperturbation to transit the northern into central Rockies and has \nbeen steadily working over western portions of the area. This, along \nwith return flow and a weak LLJ overnight have allowed for a WAA \nregime to keep a well developed area of showers with a few embedded \nthunderstorms going as they steadily move east. Mid level lapse \nrates have fallen below 6 C/km which has coincided with a drop in \noverall lightning production through the entire night into early \nmorning hours. A few areas over central into north central Kansas \nexperience high rainfall totals earlier last evening into the early \novernight period which resulted in a few flood related warnings and \nadvisories. From, here it looks like the high rainfall amounts have \nbeen had. Weak isentropic ascent eventually wanes into midday as the \nupper wave transitions overhead and east of the area into this \nafternoon. Thus expecting clouds to linger even after the showers \ncome to an end by midday in the early afternoon.  \n\nSaturday morning could see some fog form across the area as skies \nclear tomorrow night and winds remain calm. Wouldn't rule out some \nareas of patchy dense fog but confidence in coverage is low right \nnow so have held off mention.  \n\nHeat then becomes the primary forecast headline into Saturday \nthrough Tuesday across the area. The western ridge builds once again \nand intensifies Sunday and Monday especially with H85 temperatures \nrising to around 30C which will help push air temp values to near \ntriple digits in some areas with ensemble data and NBM percentiles \nshowing little spread leading to high confidence in heat headlines \nbeing necessary once again. Have opted to go with an Extreme Heat \nWatch at this time due to the duration and intensity of the expected \nheat.  Will likely need 4 days of heat headlines across the entire \narea or at least most of the area heading into Tuesday. \nUnfortunately, there appears to be little relief from this heat wave \nas the cool down only takes heat index values to the upper 90s which \nmay feel cool by comparison into next weekend. Any modified cold \nfront on the easter flank of the semi-permanent western ridge is \nlikely too weak to matter much to change the overall airmass \ncharacteristics and likely mainly dry as well.",
